# Nevermore — Personal Use Licence

**Copyright © 2026 Brooks Cutter. All rights reserved.**

_Last updated: 26 July 2026_

This source code is published so anyone can read it. Nevermore reads your
mailbox and holds a credential to it, and asking for that trust while hiding
the code would be unreasonable. Publishing it is **not** a grant of the rights
an open-source licence would give you — this is source-available software, not
open source.

## What you may do

1. **Read, inspect, and audit** this source code, in full, for any purpose.
2. **Build and run** it on computers you personally own or control, for your
   own personal, non-commercial use.
3. **Modify it privately**, for your own use on your own machines, including to
   fix a bug or to satisfy yourself about what it does.
4. **Quote short excerpts** in reviews, articles, security reports, or
   educational material, with attribution.

## What you may not do

1. **Redistribute** this software, in source or binary form, modified or not,
   including on any app store, package manager, or download site.
2. **Publish a fork** or otherwise make a modified version available to others.
3. **Use it commercially** — inside a business, as part of a paid service, or
   in anything you charge for.
4. **Remove or obscure** these terms, the copyright notice, or the app's
   identity in any copy you make.

Rights not expressly granted above are reserved. In particular, no trademark
licence is granted to the name "Nevermore" or its icon.

## Contributions

Contributions are welcome but, by submitting one, you assign copyright in it to
the copyright holder, or licence it on terms that let the project relicense it
freely. This keeps the project able to ship on app stores under a single owner.
If that isn't acceptable to you, open an issue describing the change instead of
sending a patch.

## No warranty

THIS SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED "AS IS", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR
IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,
FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE, AND NONINFRINGEMENT.

Nevermore acts on your mailbox: it sends unsubscribe requests to third parties
and moves messages to your mail provider's Trash. It is designed to be
recoverable — it never issues an IMAP `EXPUNGE`, and deleted mail remains in
your provider's Trash under that provider's retention policy — but **you are
responsible for your own mail and your own backups.**

